From 9ad35ee70bf8b96149b7ad72fcf05e62b4d43724 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gaia <build@heygaia.io>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 03:39:40 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] fix(js,render): a select's selection follows HTML's
 selectedness rules everywhere

Assigning option.selected only wrote the `selected` content attribute (which
is defaultSelected) and never deselected the other options, selectedIndex
wrote a private flag its own getter ignored, and paint, :checked, FormData,
form submission and DOMSnapshot each read a different source. A page that
set option.selected saw two options flagged and the old index and value, and
Browser-Use reported a reverted selection a real browser would have made.

Selectedness is now native form state (an option's dirty selectedness, else
its attribute), and DomTree::selected_options applies HTML's selectedness
setting algorithm (a single select shows its last selected option, else its
first enabled one; a multiple select or list box shows every selected one).
option.selected, selectedIndex, value, selectedOptions, FormData, form
submission, the painted select label and DOMSnapshot's optionSelected all
read it. Selecting an option of a single select deselects the others, as
Chrome does; setting selectedness leaves the markup alone.

Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Tm2SNEJ42hMPiZ6cFb8Vih
